ABL="Ableton/MIDI-friendly: device names map to an instrument rack with parallel chains; patterns are plain MIDI clips."
EARLY="If the track has drums, they enter early (~8 counts / ~12 s); only pad/texture before that (Matt's preference)."

S("mmn6iSBKdp8","How to Make HUGE Evolving Pads with Ableton Drift",
 "velocity-to-lfo-rate-evolving-pad-smooth-wander-lfo-shape-filter-3-6-50-slow-adsr-parallel-doubler-grain-reverb-lanes-utility-trim-outer-lowpass-glue",
 "Velocity-to-LFO-rate evolving pad: a smooth slow LFO on oscillator shape and low-pass cutoff/resonance, velocity scaling the LFO rate, a 3 s / 6 s / 50% / slow-release amp envelope, and a parallel rack of dry, doubler, grain-delay and 100%-wet reverb lanes, trimmed and glued by a reverb and an outer low-pass",
 "Build a warm, slowly evolving analog-style pad from one simple subtractive synth. Use two triangle oscillators at the same octave with neutral defaults (zero any preset filter/shape offsets first). Add a slow (~0.2 Hz), smooth LFO ('wander'-type smooth random, not stepped sample-and-hold) on oscillator shape. A mod matrix sends the same LFO to low-pass cutoff and about 30% to resonance (LP ~430 Hz, HP ~40 Hz, resonance ~0.5). Velocity goes to LFO rate at full depth, so harder notes move faster. The amp envelope is 3 s attack, 6 s decay, 50% sustain and a very slow release. Then stack parallel chains in a rack: a dry lane; a doubler lane (phaser/flanger device in doubler mode, warmth up, time ~40 ms) for stereo width; a grain-delay lane (spray ~260, feedback ~30%); and a 100%-wet steady (low-modulation) reverb lane. Trim the summed gain by about 10 dB, glue everything with a reverb and then an outer low-pass auto-filter, and optionally layer a looped, crossfaded smooth foley bed. Delta vs the modulation cluster: velocity as the LFO-rate controller (performance-driven evolution) plus the parallel-lane rack layout.",
 ["A subtractive synth with 2 oscillators (triangle), a filter with HP+LP, an LFO with a smooth-random shape, a mod matrix and velocity as a mod source","A DAW rack with parallel chains; doubler/chorus, grain delay, reverb, gain utility and auto-filter devices","Optional: a sampler for a looped foley texture"],
 [("chords","midi","Slow sustained chords; velocities varied on purpose to set the movement",True),("key","string","Key of the track",False)],
 [("pad_rack","preset","Instrument rack: synth + parallel dry/doubler/grain/reverb lanes + glue reverb + outer low-pass"),("pad_audio","audio","Rendered evolving pad")],
 ["The LFO only moves timbre (oscillator shape, cutoff, resonance), never pitch. Use a smooth/wander LFO at ~0.2 Hz, not stepped sample-and-hold, so there is no pitch wobble or random note variation (Matt dislikes wobbly/random pitch).",
  "Grain-delay lane: keep grain pitch at 0 and keep the lane low in the mix. If its grains start ticking (a rapid clicking texture Matt dislikes), lower spray/feedback or mute the lane.",
  "Foley layer: the transcript used a sizzling bacon-grease sample. That is a crackle/clicking texture, so use a smooth continuous bed instead (wind, distant waves, soft rain wash), looped with a long crossfade and kept low. A bit-reduction (Redux-type) stage is optional and gentle.",
  "Gain staging: stacked parallel lanes add level, so trim about 10 dB with a utility before the glue reverb.",
  "The reverb is a steady, low-modulation large space (the transcript removed the chorus modulation from a 'large space chorus' preset), so pitch stays stable.",
  EARLY,ABL],
 [("Load a two-oscillator subtractive synth and zero any default shape/filter-drive offsets so pulling the filter down actually darkens the sound. Save this as the default.","Synth init","Neutral starting patch"),
  ("Set both oscillators to triangle at the same octave and balance their levels in the mixer.","Oscillators","Warm, soft base tone"),
  ("Route the LFO to oscillator shape at 100%, rate ~0.2 Hz, and choose a smooth random ('wander') shape. Avoid sample-and-hold, which sounds 'computery'.","LFO -> shape","Slow, smooth timbre drift while a key is held"),
  ("Filter: high-pass ~40 Hz, low-pass ~430 Hz, resonance ~0.5. In the mod matrix, send the LFO to low-pass cutoff and about 30% to low-pass resonance.","Filter + mod matrix","Dark lower-mid pad whose brightness breathes"),
  ("Map velocity to LFO rate at full depth so harder-played notes evolve faster. Vary the MIDI velocities in the chord clip on purpose.","Mod matrix: velocity -> LFO rate","Movement responds to playing dynamics"),
  ("Amp envelope '3-6-50-slow': attack 3 s, decay 6 s, sustain 50%, very slow release.","Amp envelope","Notes swell in slowly and drift away"),
  ("Group the synth into a rack and build parallel chains: (a) dry; (b) doubler (phaser/flanger in doubler mode, warmth max, time ~40 ms) for strong stereo sides; (c) grain delay (spray ~260, feedback ~30%, pitch 0); (d) steady large reverb at 100% wet. Balance lanes with the chain faders.","Rack parallel chains","Thick, wide, textured pad that keeps its centre image"),
  ("Add a utility after the lanes and pull about 10 dB of gain, then place a large reverb across everything (glue) and an auto-filter low-pass after the whole rack, pulling the cutoff down to taste.","Utility + reverb + auto-filter","Cohesive, pushed-back pad with no clipping"),
  ("Optional: in an outer rack chain, add a looped smooth foley bed in a sampler (loop on, long crossfade), lightly bit-reduced, mixed low under the pad.","Sampler loop","Organic texture layer under the pad")],
 ["Pitch-detect on a held note is stable (within 5 cents) across 20 s; spectral centroid drifts slowly (LFO on shape/filter).","Two renders of the same chord at velocity 40 and 120 show faster spectral movement at 120.","Amp envelope reaches peak around 3 s after note-on.","Side/mid energy ratio rises clearly when the doubler lane is on; mono fold-down keeps the centre (dry lane).","No transient spikes or ticks are detected from the grain/foley lanes."],
 ["pad","evolving","velocity-modulation","lfo","subtractive","parallel-fx","doubler","grain-delay","reverb","ambient"])

S("0IgwdHg0ZwM","How to not screw up your kickdrums!",
 "kick-heart-sine-sweep-goldilocks-sweep-time-not-clicky-not-laser-decay-leaves-silence-for-bass-low-start-freq-55hz-end-reference-clean-heart-parallel-saturation-layered-transient",
 "Kick 'heart' sweep rules: a pitch-swept sine whose sweep time is just right (too short = disconnected click, too long = laser), whose decay leaves silence for the bass, whose start frequency stays modest and whose end frequency is set against a ~55 Hz reference; keep the heart clean, then add saturation in parallel and an optional layered transient",
 "Synthesize the core of a kick drum (its 'heart') as a sine wave that starts high, sweeps quickly down and settles at a resting frequency, and tune it by ear with four rules. (1) Decay: long enough to thump but short enough to leave some silence before the next hit, where the bass/rumble lives. (2) Sweep time: Goldilocks. Too short and you hear a mid click plus a separate sub tone that don't connect. Too long and it sounds like a 'pew' laser. Just right gives one coherent knock. (3) Start frequency: brightness comes from it, but the heart doesn't need to reach several kHz, because saturation and layers carry the top. (4) End frequency: the perceived pitch. Use a 909-style ~55 Hz as a reference and go slightly lower for a modern feel, but not so low that small systems lose it. Keep the heart clean and undistorted, then blend saturation in parallel and optionally layer a short transient sample for 'something to hang on to' up to ~9-10 kHz. Delta vs the drum-synthesis cluster's kick variants: an explicit sweep-time diagnostic (click vs laser vs connected knock) and decay-silence rule, which are also the main levers for a softer, non-clicky kick.",
 ["A kick synth or any synth with a sine oscillator, a pitch envelope (start/end frequency, sweep time) and an amp decay","A spectrum analyser, a saturator with dry/wet or a parallel chain, and optionally a short transient sample"],
 [("tempo_bpm","number","Track tempo (sets how much silence a given decay leaves)",True),("style","string","e.g. soft/felt ambient kick vs driving techno kick",False)],
 [("kick_heart","audio","Clean pitch-swept sine kick core"),("kick","audio","Heart + parallel saturation + optional transient layer")],
 ["Clicky failure mode: a sweep that is too fast (or a start frequency that is too high) gives a disconnected click. That is the texture Matt dislikes, so for ambient tracks lean slightly toward a longer sweep and a modest start frequency. Stop before it turns into a 'laser'.",
  "The heart stays clean and undistorted; saturation is blended in parallel, not driven hard in series.",
  "The transient layer is optional. For a soft/felt ambient kick leave it out or keep it dark and low (no bright click layer).",
  "Check the kick on several systems and A/B against reference tracks; it's easy to lose perspective.",
  "This is a fixed pitch envelope that repeats identically on every hit, with no random pitch.",
  EARLY,"Original synthesis recipe; not affiliated with any kick-synth vendor. Any synth with a sine and a pitch envelope works (Web Audio: OscillatorNode frequency exponentialRampToValueAtTime + GainNode decay)."],
 [("Start from a plain sine oscillator with a pitch envelope rather than a 909-style preset, and watch it on a spectrum analyser.","Kick synth / sine + pitch envelope","A clean sweep visible from high to low"),
  ("Set the amp decay so the kick thumps but leaves some silence before the next hit at your tempo, the space where the bassline or rumble will sit.","Amp decay","Gap visible on the analyser between hits"),
  ("Adjust the sweep time (time to reach the resting frequency) by ear. If you hear a mid click and a separate sub tone, lengthen it. If it sounds like a 'pew' laser, shorten it. Stop where it feels like one connected knock.","Pitch-envelope time","Punchy, coherent knock with no detached click"),
  ("Set the start frequency only as high as needed; very high starts (toward 5 kHz) don't add bang. Leave the brightness to saturation and layers, then re-check sweep and decay.","Pitch-envelope start","Heart stays simple and focused"),
  ("Set the end (resting) frequency, i.e. the perceived kick pitch. Use ~55 Hz (909 reference) as a starting point, go a little lower for a modern feel, and check it is still audible on small speakers.","Pitch-envelope end","Kick pitch chosen deliberately"),
  ("Blend saturation in parallel (not hard in series) to add harmonics, keeping the clean heart intact.","Parallel saturator","Fuller, more audible kick without a smeared low end"),
  ("Optionally layer a short transient/top sample so the ear has something up to ~9-10 kHz in a busy mix. Balance levels so the thump stays dominant. Then keep refining end frequency and decay against the full mix.","Layer + levels","Kick that reads in a dense mix")],
 ["Pitch trace shows a single monotonic downward sweep that settles at the chosen end frequency (e.g. 50-55 Hz) on every hit.","Envelope reaches near-silence (< -40 dB) before the next beat at the chosen tempo.","No energy spike above ~3 kHz in the first 5 ms of the clean heart (no detached click)."],
 ["kick","drum-synthesis","pitch-envelope","sine","punch","saturation","soft-kick","layering"])
